@page "/guide"

<PageTitle>Workflow Guide</PageTitle>

<h1 class="h4 fw-semibold">Workflow Guide</h1>
<p class="text-muted">End-to-end map of the Studio pipeline from finding a video through review, publish, and sync maintenance.</p>

<article class="content px-4">
    <div class="card mt-3">
        <div class="card-header">
            <h2 class="h5 fw-semibold mb-0">Start to Finish</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <ol class="mb-0 ps-3">
                <li class="mb-3">
                    <a href="/">Home</a> shows the pipeline dashboard. Check it first when you want a quick read on what is harvested, approved, published, or still newer locally.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-3">
                    <a href="/creators">Creators</a> is optional. Curate the channels and playlists you revisit often so Harvest can offer quick-picks instead of requiring pasted URLs each time.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-3">
                    <a href="/harvest">Harvest + Distill</a> is where new work begins. Browse a channel or playlist, or paste a video URL, harvest the transcript, and distill it into KB notes and prompt artifacts.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-3">
                    <a href="/review">Review</a> is the approval gate. Inspect the distilled entry, then approve what is ready to ship or reject what should stay out of the publish path.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-3">
                    <a href="/publish">Publish</a> exports approved content to the private KB root named by <code>DECKFLOW_KB_ROOT</code>. Review it and commit it by hand when needed.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-3">
                    <a href="/data-coverage">Data Coverage</a> compares live rows' stored body hash to the files on <code>/data</code>.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-3">
                    <a href="/git-body-coverage">Git Body Coverage</a> compares stored body hashes to the private KB root.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-3">
                    <a href="/direct-push">Direct Push to Production</a> is the alternate publish path when you need to write approved entries straight to prod. It uploads artifacts to <code>/data</code>, writes prod DB rows, exports the private KB root, then makes rows visible.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-3">
                    Verify and maintain with <a href="/pull-from-prod">Pull from Prod</a> and <a href="/reconcile">Reconcile</a>. Use them to sync local state and audit drift.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Support pages <a href="/skipped">Skipped</a> and <a href="/blocked">Blocked</a> manage videos you do not want harvested. Skip is reversible housekeeping; block is the stronger exclusion until you unblock it.
                </li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card mt-3">
        <div class="card-header">
            <h2 class="h5 fw-semibold mb-0">Choose a Publish Path</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <div class="alert alert-info mb-3">
                <span class="oi oi-info me-1" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <strong>Safe default:</strong> <a href="/publish">Publish</a> writes the private KB root for a hand-reviewed commit. <strong>Direct Push</strong> writes live production and should be used deliberately.
            </div>
            <div class="list-group">
                <div class="list-group-item">
                    <strong>5a. Private-root publish:</strong> use <a href="/publish">Publish</a> to export <code>DECKFLOW_KB_ROOT</code>, review it, and commit it by hand.
                </div>
                <div class="list-group-item">
                    <strong>5b. Direct to prod:</strong> use <a href="/direct-push">Direct Push to Production</a> when you intentionally want the staged production write flow: upload artifacts to <code>/data</code>, update prod rows, export the private KB root, then publish visibility.
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</article>
